+## 14. Round 2 — the GCC 16.2 compatibility claim, made testable
+
+Section 13 left the tree claiming `gcc version 16.2.0 (lccc)` while a large part
+of that claim was unverified. This section closes the measurable part of the gap
+and, where it cannot close a gap, **bounds the claim explicitly** instead of
+leaving it implied. Everything below is reproducible from the commands quoted.
+
+### 14.1 The gate
+
+`tests/regression/check_gcc16_compat.py` is the acceptance gate. It takes a real
+GCC 16.2 oracle (`GCC=/home/user/oracles/gcc-16.2.0/gcc-16/bin/gcc-16`) and
+compares the two compilers on **50 checks** in eight groups: identity,
+predefined macros, `__has_builtin`, `__has_attribute`, `__has_feature`,
+diagnostics, driver queries, and language constructs.
+
+    $ GCC=.../gcc-16 python3 tests/regression/check_gcc16_compat.py
+    gcc16 compatibility: PASS (50 checks against GCC 16.2.0)
+
+It started this round at **39 of 46 failing** and is now green. The important
+design change is not the score: it is `KNOWN_OPEN_BUILTINS`,
+`KNOWN_OPEN_ATTRIBUTES`, `KNOWN_OPEN_FEATURES` and `KNOWN_OPEN_LANGUAGE`. Every
+remaining gap carries an id and a one-line reason, the gate **prints the whole
+list on every run**, and a gap that is *not* on the list fails. Debt is
+therefore visible in every CI log and a new gap cannot hide behind an old one.
+A gate that silently narrows its own scope is worse than no gate; this one
+cannot.
+
+### 14.2 Defects found and fixed this round
+
+Each entry names the evidence, because every one of them was found by a
+measurement rather than by reading the code.
+
+1. **`__attribute__((aligned))` was a silent no-op — an ABI defect.**
+   `struct __attribute__((aligned)) s { char c; };` is 16 bytes with
+   `__alignof__` 16 for GCC 16.2 and was **1/1** here. GCC documents bare
+   `aligned` (and the empty `aligned()`) as BIGGEST_ALIGNMENT. Any struct with
+   such a member, any `sizeof` in a constant expression, and any object passed
+   by value across a GCC-compiled boundary was laid out differently. Found by
+   the corpus differential below, via
+   `tests/corpus/clang-c/Sema/struct-packed-align.c`, whose
+   `extern int e1_2[sizeof(struct as1_2) == 16 ? 1 : -1];` became a negative
+   array bound. Fixed in `src/frontend/parser/parse.rs` (`aligned` arm) using
+   the existing `common::types::target_biggest_alignment()`, which already
+   tracks `-mavx`/`-mavx512f`. New gate:
+   `tests/regression/check_bare_aligned_layout.sh` — it compares *layouts*
+   against a running oracle, checks `offsetof` (not just the type's own
+   alignment field, so the attribute is proven to reach the layout pass), and
+   pins `BIGGEST_ALIGNMENT` at 16/32/64 for `-mavx`/`-mavx512f`:
+
+       bare: size=16 align=16 | aligned(8): size=8 | plain: size=1   # oracle
+       bare: size=16 align=16 | aligned(8): size=8 | plain: size=1   # lccc
+
+2. **Four libc builtin aliases were missing.**
+   `__builtin_index`, `__builtin_rindex`, `__builtin_vsprintf`,
+   `__builtin_vsnprintf` resolved to *implicit declarations*, so a program using
+   them compiled into a call to a symbol no library defines and failed at link —
+   the same failure shape as the `__builtin_<fn>f` aliases documented earlier.
+   Registered in `src/frontend/sema/builtins.rs` **and** therefore answered by
+   `__has_builtin` (the preprocessor resolver consults the same registry, so the
+   two can no longer drift apart).
+
+3. **Eight attributes that work were under-reported by `__has_attribute`.**
+   `returns_twice`, `nothrow`, `simd`, `cdecl`, `stdcall`, `unavailable`,
+   `indirect_return`, plus the guarded spellings. All eight were already
+   *accepted* by the parser; `__has_attribute` answered 0. That is the
+   dangerous direction — glibc-shaped code writes
+
+       #if __has_attribute(returns_twice)
+       # define __THROW_TWICE __attribute__((returns_twice))
+       #else
+       # define __THROW_TWICE
+       #endif
+
+   and a false answer silently drops the attribute, after which a
+   `setjmp`/`vfork` caller is optimised as if the call returned once. Each name
+   was verified accepted by both compilers before being listed.
+
+4. **`__has_feature` answered a hardcoded 0 for everything.**
+   Safe, but it made `<stddef.h>`-style feature tests take fallback paths for
+   features that exist. It now answers from a table, and — this is the part
+   that matters — **a claim is only allowed to be 1 if a witness compiles**:
+   `check_has_feature` compiles `_Alignas` and `_Static_assert` for the two
+   names it claims. `attribute_deprecated_with_message` and
+   `attribute_unavailable_with_message` stay **0** because the diagnostics do
+   not exist yet (see TODO-23): `__attribute__((deprecated("msg")))` is parsed
+   and then never warns, and GCC 16 makes a call to an `unavailable` function an
+   *error*. Advertising those two would be a lie of exactly the kind `_Countof`
+   was.
+
+5. **Earlier in the round, from the same gate:** null-pointer-constant
+   acceptance (`int *p = 0;`), array-to-pointer decay on the conversion path,
+   `conflicting types` on incompatible redeclarations, and `_Countof`
+   (GCC 15) — the last one having been *masked* by the demoted
+   implicit-declaration warning, which is the single most useful lesson of the
+   round: **a construct that "works" because the diagnostic that would have
+   caught it was demoted is not implemented, it is hidden.**
+
