@@ -0,0 +1,109 @@
+//! The compiler-version claim, in exactly one place.
+//!
+//! # Why this is a module and not six literals
+//!
+//! The version a compiler reports is not a label, it is a *switch*: every
+//! `__GNUC_PREREQ` in every header, every `#if GCC_VERSION >= …` in the Linux
+//! kernel and every `AC_PROG_CC`-derived flag in a configure script branches on
+//! it.  A compiler that answers `__GNUC__ == 16` while `-dumpversion` prints
+//! `14` does not merely look inconsistent -- it makes a build system enable
+//! the flags it enables for GCC 16 and configure for GCC 14.
+//!
+//! The same drift keeps recurring in this tree, so the claim is centralised:
+//! the preprocessor macros, the driver's `-dumpversion`/`-dumpfullversion`,
+//! the `--version` and `-v` banners, and every test that pins them read these
+//! constants.  Updating the claim is then a one-line change here plus whatever
+//! the claim newly promises -- which is exactly what it should be.
+
+/// Major version of the GCC release whose behaviour we implement.
+pub(crate) const GCC_MAJOR: u32 = 16;
+
+/// Minor version of that release.
+pub(crate) const GCC_MINOR: u32 = 2;
+
+/// Patchlevel of that release.
+pub(crate) const GCC_PATCHLEVEL: u32 = 0;
+
+/// The full `major.minor.patchlevel` string, as `__VERSION__` and
+/// `-dumpfullversion` report it.
+///
+/// Kept as one literal rather than built from the numbers so a test can assert
+/// the two forms agree; building it here would make the assertion vacuous.
+pub(crate) const GCC_VERSION: &str = "16.2.0";
+
+/// What `-dumpversion` prints.
+///
+/// GCC's `-dumpversion` prints the version component of the compiler's
+/// *filesystem paths*, which is a configure-time choice
+/// (`--with-gcc-major-version-only`, which Debian and Ubuntu use, versus the
+/// upstream default).  Distributions print the bare major version and their
+/// paths use it, so lccc prints the major version and `-dumpfullversion` serves
+/// the callers that need all three components.  This is the behaviour a
+/// project sees from a distribution GCC 16, which is the one it will compare
